eet (SDS)\nL150: \nL151: R2A Broth\nL152: \nL153: #### Electronic Instructions for Use (eIFU)\nL154: \nL155: R2A Broth\nL156: \nL157: #### R2A Broth\nL158: \nL159: Certification of Analysis\nL160: \nL161: ####\nL162: \nL163: Available Stock\nL164: ### Stock Checkout (M1687)\nL165: \nL166: Material Number Material Description Batch Number Stockkeeping Units Qty Shelf Life Expiration\nL167: \nL168: 【74†Image: User】 Your enquiry has been submitted\nL169: \nL170: Something problem with the server. Try again.\nL171: \nL172: R2A Broth\nL173: \nL174: Please enter the enquiry message\nL175: \nL176: I have read and accept the Privacy Policy\nL177: \nL178: This is a required field.\nL179: \nL180:   Submit\nL181: \nL182: Available Stock\nL183: \nL184: Please 【42†login】 to use Stock Checkout.\nL185: \nL186: Please 【42†login】 to Post an Enquiry.\nL187: \nL188:  Please 【43†Login】 to Post an Enquiry.\nL189: Enquiry sent. We will get back to you soon!\nL190: \nL191: Something problem with the server. Try again.\nL192: ## Drinking Water#CC293D\nL193: \nL194:  【44† Description 】\nL195: \nL196: R-2A Broth is used for cultivation and maintenance of heterotrophic bacteria from potable waters.\nL197: \nL198: ### Composition**\nL199: \nL200: Ingredients Gms / Litre\nL201: Casein acid hydrolysate 0.500\nL202: Yeast extract 0.500\nL203: Proteose peptone 0.500\nL204: Dextrose 0.500\nL205: Starch, soluble 0.500\nL206: Dipotassium phosphate 0.300\nL207: Magnesium sulphate 0.024\nL208: Sodium pyruvate 0.300\nL209: \nL210: Final pH ( at 25°C) 7.2±0.2\nL211: \nL212: **Formula adjusted, standardized to suit performance parameters\nL213: ### Directions\nL214: \nL215: Suspend 3.12 grams in 1000 ml distilled water. Heat if necessary to dissolve the medium completely. Dispense into tubes. Sterilize by autoclaving at 15 lbs pressure (121°C) for 15 minutes. DO NOT OVERHEAT.\nL216: ### Principle And Interpretation\nL217: \nL218: The total bacterial count of drinking water is determined by plate count on a nutritionally rich medium. However all organisms present are not able to grow on them, either because they are slow growers or because they cant grow on that media (1). For this reason a nutritionally reduced medium was described. R-2A Agar is a modification of this medium (2,3).\nL219: R-2A Agar is an alternative medium used for the heterotrophic plate counts and for subculturing isolates from potable waters (1). R-2A Agar is also recommended by APHA (4) for pour plate, spread plate and membrane filter technique. R-2A Broth is similar to R-2A Agar except agar. Total count recommended for the bacterial examination of potable waters gives an estimate of the aerobic and facultatively anaerobic bacteria, which grow best at 35°C in a rich medium (3).\nL220: R-2A Broth enables better recovery of these bacteria from treated waters under different incubation conditions. Many bacteria from natural waters, which contain limited nutrients at ambient temperature, grow best on the media with less nutrient levels. They grow better at the temperatures below the routine laboratory incubation temperatures of 35 to 37°C (3).\nL221: This medium contains casein acid hydrolysate, yeast extract, biopeptone as source of essential growth factors required for metabolism of the bacteria. Dextrose is the energy source. Starch acts as a neutralizer that neutralizes any toxic metabolites, if present. Phosphate buffers the medium while sodium pyruvate supplies additional nutrition. Magnesium sulphate serves as a source of ions.\nL222: Due to the presence of the above mentioned ingredients these media allow the growth of stressed and chlorine tolerant bacteria present in treated waters.\nL223: ### Quality Control\nL224: \nL225: Appearance Cream to yellow homogeneous free flowing powder\nL226: \nL227: Colour and Clarity of prepared medium Yellow coloured, clear solution in tubes\nL228: \nL229: Reaction Reaction of 0.312% w/v aqueous solution at 25°C. pH : 7.2±0.2\nL230: \nL231: pH 7.00-7.40\nL232: \nL233: Cultural Response M1687: Cultural characteristics observed *by using standard ATCC cultures after an incubation at 35-37°C for 24-72 hours.\nL234: (*-In case of water samples from fields it is suggested to incubate further for upto 7 days to ascertain the absence of organisms)\nL235: \nL236: Organism Inoculum (CFU) Growth\nL237: Candida albicans ATCC 10231 50-100 good-luxuriant\nL238: Enterococcus faecalis ATCC 29212 50-100 good-luxuriant\nL239: Escherichia coli ATCC 25922 50-100 good-luxuriant\nL240: Salmonella Enteritidis ATCC 13076 50-100 good-luxuriant\nL241: Salmonella Typhi ATCC 6539 50-100 good-luxuriant\nL242: ### Storage and Shelf Life\nL243: \nL244: Store below 30°C in tightly closed container and the prepared medium at 2-8°C. Use before expiry date on the label.\nL245: ### Reference\nL246: \nL247:   1. Reasoner and Geldreich, 1985, Appl. Environ. Microbiol., 49:1.\nL248:   2. Stark and McCoy. 1938. Zentralbl. Bacteriol. Parasitenkd. Infectionskr. Hyg. Abt.2 98 : 201\nL249:   3. Collins and Willoughby, 1962, Arch. Microbiol., 43:294.\nL250:   4. Greenberg A. E., Trussell R. R. and Clesceri L. S. (Eds.), 1985, Standard Methods for the Examination of Water and Wastewater, 16th ed., APHA, Washington, DC\nL251: \nL252:  【45† Product Information 】\nL253:  More Information Product Name R2A Broth\nL254: SKU M1687\nL255: Product Type Regular\nL256: Physical Form Powder\nL257: Origin Animal\nL258: Packaging type HDPE\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRacemase activity effected by two dehydrogenases in sulfolactate degradation by Chromohalobacter salexigens: purification of (S)-sulfolactate dehydrogenase | Microbiology Society (https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.034736-0)\nciteturn33view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.034736-0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 301\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 1887\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Skip to content】\nL5: \nL6: [Input]\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Microbiology】 【3†Volume 156, Issue 3】\nL9: # Racemase activity effected by two dehydrogenases in sulfolactate degradation by Chromohalobacter salexigens: purification of (S)-sulfolactate dehydrogenase 【4†Image: Free】\nL10: \nL11:   * 【5†Karin Denger】^{1} and 【6†Alasdair M. Cook】^{1}\nL12:   * 【7†View Affiliations Hide Affiliations】\nL13: \nL14: ^{1} 【8†Department of Biology, The University, D-78457 Konstanz, Germany】\nL15: \nL16: Correspondence Alasdair M. Cook\nL17:  【9†[email protected]†uni-konstanz.de】\nL18: \nL19:   * Published: 01 March 2010\nL20:   * 【7†info Info】\nL21:     * 【10†Article information】\nL22:     * 【11†Full-Text】\nL23:     * 【12†Figures and Tables】\nL24:     * 【13†References (36)】\nL25:     * 【14†Cited By (56)】\nL26:     * 【15†Supplementary Material】\nL27:     * 【16†Metrics】\nL28: \nL29:   * 【7†build Tools】\nL30: ### Tools\nL31: \nL32:     * 【7†Add to my favourites】\nL33: \nL34:     * 【7†Create publication alert】\nL35: \nL36: 【7†Create citation alert】\nL37: \nL38: 【7†Create correction alert】\nL39: \nL40:     * 【7†Export citation】\nL41:       * 【17†BibT_{E}X】\nL42:       * 【18†Endnote】\nL43:       * 【17†Zotero】\nL44:       * 【19†Medlars】\nL45:       * 【20†RefWorks】\nL46:       * 【21†Mendeley†www.mendeley.com】\nL47:     * 【7†Recommend to library】\nL48:     *     * [Input]\nL49: ## Abstract\nL50: Chromohalobacter salexigens DSM 3043, whose genome has been sequenced, is known to degrade (R,S)-sulfolactate as a sole carbon and energy source for growth. Utilization of the compound(s) was shown to be quantitative, and an eight-gene cluster (Csal_1764–Csal_1771) was hypothesized to encode the enzymes in the degradative pathway.\nL51: It comprised a transcriptional regulator (SuyR), a Tripartite Tricarboxylate Transporter-family uptake system for sulfolactate (SlcHFG), two sulfolactate dehydrogenases of opposite sulfonate stereochemistry, namely novel SlcC and ComC [(R)-sulfolactate dehydrogenase] [EC1.1.1.272] and desulfonative sulfolactate sulfo-lyase (SuyAB) [EC4.4.1.24]. Inducible reduction of 3-sulfopyruvate, inducible SuyAB activity and induction of an unknown protein were detected.\nL52: Separation of the soluble proteins from induced cells on an anion-exchange column yielded four relevant fractions. Two different fractions reduced sulfopyruvate with NAD(P)H, a third yielded SuyAB activity, and the fourth contained the unknown protein. The latter was identified by peptide-mass fingerprinting as SlcH, the candidate periplasmic binding protein of the transport system. Separated SuyB was also identified by peptide-mass fingerprinting.\nL53: ComC was partially purified and identified by peptide-mass fingerprinting. The (R)-sulfolactate that ComC produced from sulfopyruvate was a substrate for SuyAB, which showed that SuyAB is (R)-sulfolactate sulfo-lyase. SlcC was purified to homogeneity. This enzyme also formed sulfolactate from sulfopyruvate, but the latter enantiomer was not a substrate for SuyAB. SlcC was obviously (S)-sulfolactate dehydrogenase.\nL54:   * Received: 16/09/2009\nL55:   * Accepted: 09/12/2009\nL56:   * Revised: 02/12/2009\nL57:   * Published Online: 01/03/2010\nL58: \nL59: Keyword(s): 【22†ComC, (R)-sulfolactate dehydrogenase】 , 【23†SlcC, (S)-sulfolactate dehydrogenase】 , 【24†SlcHFG, tripartite tricarboxylate transport (TTT)-family transporter for sulfolactate】 , 【25†SQ, sulfoquinovose】 and 【26†SuyAB, sulfolactate sulfo-lyase】\nL60: \nL61: SGM\nL62: \nL63: 【27†Image: Loading】\nL64: \nL65: Article metrics loading...\nL66: \nL67: /content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.034736-0\nL68: \nL69: 2010-03-01\nL70: \nL71: 2026-10-21\nL72: ## Metrics\nL73: \nL74: 【27†Image: Loading full text...】\nL75: \nL76: Full text loading...\nL77: \nL78: /deliver/fulltext/micro/156/3/967.html?itemId=/content/journal/micro/10.1099/mic.0.034736-0&mimeType=html&fmt=ahah\nL79: \